course: dictionary
lesson: 7947
title: Rebellion: fighting back against power
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A rebellion is when people refuse to obey the people in charge, such as a ruler or a government. Sometimes they fight against them. Use the words around the word to find its meaning: «The ruler's new taxes made the people angry, and they rose in rebellion.» The people would not accept the taxes, so rebellion means they fought against the ruler or refused to obey.
explain: «The Yuan ended when floods, famine and rebellions shook it.» Floods and famine are serious disasters, so rebellions must be serious trouble too, something that shakes a ruler's power. Check your meaning by putting it into the sentence: "fighting against the ruler" still makes sense, so you have it right.
explain: Rebellion can also be small. «A bit of rebellion» at home means breaking a small rule, like a teenager ignoring a bedtime. The same word works at two sizes, a whole country or one family, and the clues in the sentence show which size is meant.
fact: Rebellion is a noun, and it is usually uncountable: you can say "rebellion" as an idea, or "a rebellion" as one event.
fact: «Rebellion» means refusing to obey, or fighting against, the people in power.
fact: «Lead a rebellion» means to start it and be in charge of it, and «crush a rebellion» means to defeat it.
fact: The Taiping Rebellion, 1850–1864, was led by Hong Xiuquan, and it was one of the deadliest wars in history.
fact: A rebellion can be big, like a war against a ruler, or small, like «an act of rebellion» against a rule at school or home.
exercise: «The Ming also rebuilt the Great Wall, but famine and rebellion inside weakened it.» What does rebellion mean here? | A kind of trade in silk | A royal birthday party | A long wall built of brick and stone | *Groups of people fighting against the rulers
exercise: What does «crush a rebellion» mean? | *To defeat it | To hide it in a box | To start it with a big party | To write a song about it
exercise: A child breaks a small rule at home. Which phrase describes this? | A bit of rain | A bit of sleep | A bit of cake | *A bit of rebellion
test: «People believed Heaven took away the right to rule when floods, famine and rebellion came.» What does rebellion most likely mean here? | *People fighting against or refusing to obey the ruler | A wall made of stone | A big family meal | A kind of cloud over the sea
test: A teenager refuses to obey a bedtime rule. Is this a rebellion? | Only if the teenager is a ruler | *Yes, it is a small act of rebellion | No, rebellion means being happy | No, rebellion only means a war
exam: Which sentence uses rebellion correctly? | She rebelled her sandwich at lunch. | The rebellion was a delicious chocolate cake. | The rebellion of the sun set at six o'clock. | *The villagers rose in rebellion against the unfair tax.
